  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...    \nEnsembles remain in good agreement that longwave troughing will \nbe maintained off the West Coast through the upcoming work week \ninto next weekend, as a series of potent upper lows (centers of \nstrongly negative midlevel height anomalies) dive \nsouth/southeastward from the Gulf of Alaska and fill out the base \nof the longwave trough. These systems are then advertised to eject\neastward over the Western US, albeit in a weakening phase as they\nprogress inland. The first of these systems is still shown moving\nthrough the region Monday night through Tuesday night, dragging \nthe southern extent of a cold front over the forecast area during \nthe day on Tuesday. Ascent mechanisms ahead of the low will be \nsufficient to produce at least scattered shower activity and may \nrelease modest elevated instability up to around 100-250 J/kg, \nresulting in low chances for isolated/embedded convection \n(lightning). GFS deterministic guidance does show some weak \nfrontogenetic forcing present as well, which could serve as a \nfocus for a band of showers/embedded convective activity Tuesday \nafternoon across a portion of Southern and Central AZ. Latest \nWPC/NBM QPF has come down from previous forecasts, though it is \ntough to say if this is a trend or just model variation. \nRegardless, NBM 25th-75th percentiles for Phoenix are now between\n0.05\"-0.50\", with rain totals most likely averaging 0.10\"-0.25\" \nand higher totals focused over foothills and south/southwest \nfacing slopes to the north and northeast of Phoenix. \nEmbedded/isolated convection could produce locally higher totals. \n\nIn addition to the rain chances, Tuesday's system will result in \ndecent height packing and thus fairly widespread breezy \nconditions. The terrain gap along I-8 in far southwest Imperial \nCounty will be most susceptible strong winds, with gusts likely \nexceeding 40-55 mph, and so a Wind Advisory may be needed in \nfuture updates. \n\nConditions will quickly dry on the backside of this first system,\nwith ensemble mean PWATs dropping closer to seasonal averages \n(0.40-0.50\"). Another transient ridge will likely take hold and \nallow temperatures to moderate closer to normal before another \nPacific low impinges on the Southwest US late in the week. Model \nconsensus remains that this next system will have a track further \nto the north, and thus PoPs and QPF remain fairly low and confined\nmostly to AZ higher terrain areas, however, discrepancies in \ntiming are still apparent, and the track is not certain. The \ntemperature forecast has come into better agreement late week into\nnext weekend, with NBM IQRs now below 5F. Forecast highs drop \ninto a middle 60s to middle 70s range across the lower deserts \nlate week into next weekend, at least a few degrees below daily \nnormals.",
